  "query_preview": "..\n(This evening through next Monday)\nIssued at 1250 PM CDT Tue Jul 28 2026\n\nMidday temperatures across the Mid-South are 2-4 degrees warmer\nthan yesterday. Satellite imagery shows high clouds streaming\nfrom the north, which may limit afternoon heating. However, as\ntemperatures already exceed yesterday's readings, no significant\nchanges will be made to the afternoon package. Expect highs in\nthe upper 90s to near 100 degrees, with heat indices reaching\ndangerous triple digits. An Extreme Heat Warning remains in\neffect through 8 PM this evening for the entire Mid-South.\n\nMost of the area will see relief from oppressive heat starting\nWednesday as a cold front traverses the region from northeast to\nsouthwest. CAMs have been inconsistent regarding the spatial and\ntemporal distribution of precipitation associated with the front.\nWhile latest guidance remains dry, the presence of a boundary and\ndew points in the 70s may generate isolated showers. Although\ntemperatures will be slightly cooler area-wide, pooling dew\npoints ahead of the front could boost heat indices into the\ntriple digits again. Portions of the Delta, east Arkansas, and\nnortheast Mississippi may approach Heat Advisory criteria (105+\ndegrees) tomorrow.\n\nFrom tomorrow through Friday, a 595+ dam ridge over the Rockies\nwill slowly shift east over the Plains while weakening slightly.\nA deepening trough along the East Coast will result in\nstrengthening north to northwest flow across the middle and lower\nMississippi River Valley. A cold front associated with a strong\nshortwave tracking across the Midwest will move into the Mid-\nSouth late Friday or Saturday, providing the best chance for\nwidespread rainfall in the medium range. Guidance indicates a\nsurface low may develop in the Plains and track across the Middle\nMississippi River Valley by Saturday. Depending on its track, a\nfew stronger thunderstorms are possible late Friday into\nSaturday. While the Storm Prediction Center has not yet\nhighlighted the Mid-South for severe weather, the situation bears\nmonitoring. Confidence in cooler temperatures through the weekend\nremains high, with highs near or slightly below normal (generally\nat or below 90 degrees) and lows in the upper 60s to middle 70s.",
